1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,550
Chai Corner now has five outlets,
and Ravi's phone never stops.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:07,205
Milk is low at the market outlet.

3
00:00:07,205 --> 00:00:08,105
Ask Ravi.

4
00:00:08,337 --> 00:00:10,464
A customer wants a refund.

5
00:00:10,464 --> 00:00:11,407
Ask Ravi.

6
00:00:11,707 --> 00:00:14,633
And a big new tea shop
opens near the college.

7
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:18,293
Forty questions a day, and
no time left for the big one.

8
00:00:18,943 --> 00:00:21,143
Not every decision is the same.

9
00:00:21,143 --> 00:00:22,916
Ravi starts sorting them.

10
00:00:23,616 --> 00:00:26,321
Picture the festival
rush at the main outlet.

11
00:00:26,671 --> 00:00:27,955
The queue is long.

12
00:00:27,955 --> 00:00:30,947
Asha pours the chai, and
Kabir takes the money.

13
00:00:31,297 --> 00:00:35,157
Every few minutes, someone asks
Ravi the same small things.

14
00:00:35,157 --> 00:00:36,057
More cups?

15
00:00:36,018 --> 00:00:37,651
Change for a big note?

16
00:00:38,001 --> 00:00:41,791
Ravi answers them again and again,
while the big questions wait.

17
00:00:42,441 --> 00:00:44,410
So, how do we sort decisions?

18
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:49,992
In 1960, Herbert Simon named two
kinds, in his book, The New Science of

19
00:00:49,992 --> 00:00:51,519
Management Decision.

20
00:00:51,869 --> 00:00:54,720
Programmed decisions are
routine and repetitive.

21
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:57,070
You can write a rule for them once.

22
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:00,404
Non-programmed decisions
are new and unstructured.

23
00:01:00,404 --> 00:01:03,973
That means there is no ready
rule, and no clear path.

24
00:01:04,323 --> 00:01:07,728
So they need judgment: weighing
the facts, and thinking it through.

25
00:01:08,378 --> 00:01:10,806
Watch Ravi sort one day's questions.

26
00:01:11,156 --> 00:01:12,918
Milk below twenty litres?

27
00:01:12,918 --> 00:01:14,225
There is a rule.

28
00:01:14,225 --> 00:01:15,125
Reorder.

29
00:01:14,945 --> 00:01:15,845
Programmed.

30
00:01:16,116 --> 00:01:17,312
A wrong order?

31
00:01:17,312 --> 00:01:18,677
There is a rule.

32
00:01:18,677 --> 00:01:19,704
Full refund.

33
00:01:19,704 --> 00:01:20,614
Programmed.

34
00:01:20,914 --> 00:01:22,270
A helper is sick?

35
00:01:22,270 --> 00:01:23,878
Call the standby list.

36
00:01:23,878 --> 00:01:24,778
Programmed.

37
00:01:25,048 --> 00:01:27,513
A new tea shop near the college?

38
00:01:27,513 --> 00:01:28,686
No rule fits.

39
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:29,783
Non-programmed.

40
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:32,246
An outlet in another city?

41
00:01:32,246 --> 00:01:33,480
No rule fits.

42
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:34,633
Non-programmed.

43
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:37,958
Thirty six of forty questions are routine.

44
00:01:37,958 --> 00:01:39,611
Ravi keeps just four.

45
00:01:40,261 --> 00:01:42,937
Programmed decisions run on three tools.

46
00:01:43,287 --> 00:01:45,503
A rule says exactly what to do.

47
00:01:45,503 --> 00:01:47,849
Milk below twenty litres, reorder.

48
00:01:48,199 --> 00:01:50,710
A procedure is a set of steps, in order.

49
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:53,030
Check the bill, say sorry, pay back.

50
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:56,474
A policy is a broad guide for many cases.

51
00:01:56,474 --> 00:01:58,534
Customers always come first.

52
00:01:58,884 --> 00:02:03,027
Write it once, and anyone at any
outlet can decide in seconds.

53
00:02:03,677 --> 00:02:06,046
Non-programmed decisions are different.

54
00:02:06,396 --> 00:02:09,813
They are rare, the stakes are
high, and the facts are unclear.

55
00:02:10,163 --> 00:02:14,903
So Ravi uses the full decision
process from last time, step by step.

56
00:02:15,253 --> 00:02:18,488
And they go to the top, because
a mistake here costs a lot.

57
00:02:19,138 --> 00:02:21,260
So where do you see this at work today?

58
00:02:21,610 --> 00:02:22,510
Rules.

59
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:27,021
A bank lends a small sum only when the
customer's score passes a set mark.

60
00:02:27,371 --> 00:02:28,271
Procedures.

61
00:02:28,246 --> 00:02:31,694
Pilots run the same checklist
before every take off.

62
00:02:32,044 --> 00:02:32,944
Policies.

63
00:02:32,842 --> 00:02:36,088
A shop takes back any
item within thirty days.

64
00:02:36,438 --> 00:02:37,338
Judgment.

65
00:02:37,293 --> 00:02:41,120
A hospital plans how to
handle a brand new disease.

66
00:02:41,470 --> 00:02:42,596
And computers.

67
00:02:42,596 --> 00:02:46,594
Simon saw that routine decisions
can be handed to machines.

68
00:02:46,594 --> 00:02:49,457
A bank's computer flags odd card payments.

69
00:02:50,107 --> 00:02:51,921
So are rules always good?

70
00:02:51,921 --> 00:02:54,360
The honest answer is, yes and no.

71
00:02:54,710 --> 00:02:55,847
The good side.

72
00:02:55,847 --> 00:02:58,122
Rules are fast, cheap and fair.

73
00:02:58,122 --> 00:03:00,186
Everyone gets the same answer.

74
00:03:00,536 --> 00:03:01,712
The hard side.

75
00:03:01,712 --> 00:03:04,388
Rules go stale when the world changes.

76
00:03:04,738 --> 00:03:07,431
And a rule followed blindly can be silly.

77
00:03:07,431 --> 00:03:11,568
A regular customer lost his bill,
so the rule says, no refund.

78
00:03:11,918 --> 00:03:16,746
Simon himself saw the two types as
two ends of one line, not two boxes.

79
00:03:16,746 --> 00:03:18,708
Most choices sit in between.

80
00:03:19,058 --> 00:03:20,709
So the lesson is balance.

81
00:03:20,709 --> 00:03:24,865
Turn the routine into rules, and
save your judgment for what is new.

82
00:03:25,515 --> 00:03:26,427
Quick check.

83
00:03:26,427 --> 00:03:28,759
A storm shuts the dairy for a week.

84
00:03:28,759 --> 00:03:30,640
It has never happened before.

85
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:32,671
Which type of decision is this?

86
00:03:34,571 --> 00:03:35,597
Non-programmed!

87
00:03:35,597 --> 00:03:39,002
It is new, no rule fits,
so it needs judgment.

88
00:03:39,602 --> 00:03:40,615
So, remember.

89
00:03:40,915 --> 00:03:44,574
Programmed decisions are
routine, and handled with rules.

90
00:03:44,924 --> 00:03:48,817
Non-programmed decisions are new,
and need judgment at the top.

91
00:03:49,167 --> 00:03:52,796
Turn the routine into rules,
and save time for what is new.

92
00:03:53,396 --> 00:03:58,019
Next time, rational decision making,
and how to pick the very best option.
